    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Puerto de la Cruz to Tenerife North Airport</h2>
<p>Leaving Puerto de la Cruz for a flight is a short, predictable trip by private transfer: around 26 kilometres and roughly 25 minutes along the TF-5 motorway east to Tenerife North Airport (TFN). For travellers based on this side of the island, TFN is very often the relevant airport rather than Tenerife South, which sits considerably further away and serves a different mix of international holiday flights.</p>
<p>The distinction matters most if your next flight is a domestic route or a hop to another Canary Island rather than a long-haul departure. Tenerife North Airport is the main hub for Binter Canarias and Canaryfly, which together operate the great majority of flights connecting Tenerife to Gran Canaria, La Palma, Lanzarote, Fuerteventura, El Hierro, and La Gomera, alongside domestic routes to the Spanish mainland. If your onward journey falls into that category, you'll be departing from TFN, and a transfer from Puerto de la Cruz gets you there considerably faster than the drive to the south of the island would.</p>
<p>A private driver collects you from your hotel or address in Puerto de la Cruz at a time calculated from your flight's departure, with a sensible margin for check-in, and drives you directly east along the TF-5 through the terraced, green hillside terrain typical of this side of Tenerife. There's no need to arrange parking, work around a bus timetable, or share a shuttle that makes other stops first — just a fixed pickup and a direct run to the terminal, with traffic generally light outside the immediate rush-hour windows near the airport itself.</p>
<p>You can arrange your pickup address, flight number, and price in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>, and check your flight's status ahead of time at <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a>. If your stay in Tenerife also included other towns or the island's capital area, our <a href="/city-to-city-transfers/">city-to-city transfers</a> service can fold those legs into the same booking, ending with this transfer to the airport.</p>
<h3>About Tenerife North Airport (TFN)</h3>
<p>Tenerife North Airport, officially Tenerife North–Ciudad de La Laguna Airport and still commonly known as Los Rodeos, operates a single modern terminal rebuilt in 2002. It has handled the island's domestic and inter-island traffic since international flights moved to Tenerife South in 1978, and for travellers departing Puerto de la Cruz on any of those routes, it's the far more convenient of Tenerife's two airports.</p>
<h2>About Puerto de la Cruz</h2>
<p>Puerto de la Cruz is one of the Canary Islands' original resort towns, a traditional destination on Tenerife's north coast that was welcoming visitors long before the island's newer southern resorts took shape. That earlier history gives it a genuinely different atmosphere from purpose-built holiday developments, with an old town, working harbour, and town squares that function as real, everyday Canarian spaces rather than purely tourist infrastructure.</p>
<h3>A Final Swim at Lago Martiánez</h3>
<p>Many visitors save a last visit to Lago Martiánez for their final morning before departure, the striking seawater lido designed by César Manrique that spans roughly 100,000 square metres across seven interconnected saltwater pools, small islands, and sculptural walkways. Because Puerto de la Cruz's natural coastline is rockier and more exposed to the Atlantic than the engineered beaches further south, Manrique's design gave the town a safe, purpose-built alternative to open-water swimming directly on the seafront, and it remains as central to the town's identity today as it was when it opened.</p>
<h3>A Botanical Legacy from the 18th Century</h3>
<p>Just outside the centre, the Jardín Botánico de Puerto de la Cruz dates back to the late 18th century, founded under King Charles III as an acclimatisation garden for tropical plants travelling between the Americas and mainland Spain. That original purpose left the garden with an unusually early and substantial botanical collection for its size, including a centuries-old strangler fig that remains one of its best-known features, and it's a popular stop for visitors with a few spare hours before their driver arrives.</p>
<h3>A Town Shaped by an Earlier Era of Tourism</h3>
<p>The layered history of Puerto de la Cruz — Canarian town first, resort destination second — shows in details that newer developments further south simply don't have: older architecture around the Plaza del Charco, a working fishing harbour, and beaches like Playa Jardín and La Paz that sit alongside the town rather than defining it entirely. For visitors departing after a stay built around this slower, more traditional side of Tenerife, it's often worth allowing time on a final morning for one more walk along the seafront promenade before heading to the airport.</p>
<h3>An Early Winter Retreat for European Visitors</h3>
<p>Long before mass tourism reached the Canary Islands, Puerto de la Cruz was already attracting well-off visitors from Britain and continental Europe, drawn by a reputation for a mild, health-giving winter climate that took hold in the late 19th century. The Gran Hotel Taoro, opened in 1890 on a hillside overlooking the town, was among the grandest hotels built anywhere in the Canary Islands during that era, and its presence marked Puerto de la Cruz as one of the archipelago's earliest genuine tourist destinations, decades before the newer resort towns further south existed in any recognisable form. Traces of that period still shape the town's older streets and villas today, giving departing visitors a sense of just how long this stretch of the north coast has been welcoming travellers.</p>
<h3>Hotel and Business Transfers from Puerto de la Cruz</h3>
<p>Puerto de la Cruz's accommodation includes long-established seafront hotels that have anchored the town's tourism industry for decades, alongside smaller guesthouses in the older streets inland. There's no dedicated hotel page for this route, but a private transfer collects you from whichever hotel or address you provide when booking, regardless of whether it's a large resort property or a small pensión. The town's long history as a conference and events destination on the north coast also means a share of departures are business travellers; our <a href="/corporate-travel/">corporate travel</a> service offers the fixed pricing and punctual pickups that kind of schedule requires.</p>
<p>Families and groups travelling with extra luggage, sports equipment, or a child seat should flag this when booking so the right vehicle is arranged in advance, and larger parties departing together after a family gathering or an event tied to the town's gardens or conference facilities can request an appropriately sized vehicle through our <a href="/group-event-transfers/">group event transfers</a> service rather than splitting into multiple cars.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Whether your stay in Puerto de la Cruz was a full holiday or one stop on a wider tour of Tenerife, booking your transfer to Tenerife North Airport in advance keeps your departure simple regardless of your flight time or how far ahead you plan.</p>`,
